I'm reconsidering a cleaner design where settle() takes an explicit list of excluded depositors supplied by the caller, with the contract verifying each has maxPrice below the threshold—but I still need a way to confirm the exclusion list is complete, since nothing stops an important depositor from being left out.

Discrete price tiers solve this neatly: restrict limit prices to a small fixed set of buckets (say 8 multipliers of the initial price), and accumulate deposits per bucket during the deposit phase. At settle time, compute P₁ across all deposits, then exclude any bucket whose limit falls below P₁, subtract that from the total to get D₂, and compute P₂—all in O(8) regardless of depositor count. Then claimWindow just checks which bucket a user belongs to: refund if excluded, otherwise fill at P₂. This feels like the right deterministic, gas-efficient approach to rewrite that section around.

